            <ArticleTitle>Trichoscopy of scalp dysesthesia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>245-247</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5114/pdia.2017.67148</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Scalp dysesthesia is characterized by localized pruritus, burning sensations or even pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To describe characteristic trichoscopic features of scalp dysesthesia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The study was a retrospective analysis (2010-2016) of 9 patients (8 females and 1 male; median age: 52 years; range: 38-66 years). A total of 61 trichoscopic images were analyzed by two independent evaluators.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">In all cases, oval or round areas covered with short hairs: broom hairs (77%; 7/9), block hairs (88%; 8/9), short hairs with trichorrhexis nodosa (78%; 7/9) were found. In 4 of 9 cases these areas were circumscribed by brownish linear discoloration. Background features included: areas lacking hair shafts (44%; 4/9) covered by wavy-shaped scaling, whitish areas with prominent perifollicular yellow discoloration and cloud vessels arranged around follicular openings and around empty follicles (33%, 3/9); focal brownish discoloration with darker wavy lines (78%; 7/9).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Trichoscopy can be helpful in establishing the diagnosis of scalp dysesthesia - broom hairs, block hairs and short hairs with trichorrhexis nodosa covering the alopecia patch, uniform in length, can be considered as characteristic for this condition.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
